# The aggregate memory ceiling for the browser. Nothing runs here yet at boot;
# the two drop-ins next door - app-brave\x2dorigin@.service.d/ for the unit
# Plasma launches, app-org.chromium.Chromium-.scope.d/ for the one Chromium
# registers itself - are what put Brave into it.
#
# WHY A SLICE AT ALL, when the two drop-ins next door already carry a
# MemoryHigh each: because cgroup limits are PER-CGROUP, not per-application,
# and Brave does not run as one cgroup. Measured 2026-09-04 on a normal
# session, it runs as two at once:
#     app-brave\x2dorigin@<hex>.service       35 procs   3.44 GiB
#     app-org.chromium.Chromium-<PID>.scope     2 procs   0.80 GiB
# Plasma creates the first from the .desktop; Chromium self-registers the
# second and moves part of the tree into it. A relaunch can add a third, since
# the scope name carries the PID. A 6G cap on each of N cgroups is an N x 6G
# ceiling, which is not a ceiling. A slice caps the SUM, so the number below
# means what it says however many cgroups Chromium ends up with.
#
# NB: both drop-ins must set `Slice=browser.slice` or their cgroup is simply
# not in here and this file caps nothing. That is exactly the bug fixed on
# 2026-09-04 - the scope was in the slice, the service (95% of Brave) was not.
#
# NB: this is a USER slice (~/.config/systemd/user), not a system one. Brave's
# service and scopes both live under user@1000.service/app.slice, so the parent
# has to be in the same manager or it cannot be a parent at all.
[Unit]
Description=Browser memory ceiling

[Slice]
MemoryHigh=6G
